ntory drafted; clause-drafts could not segment the available text(s): official_fr_scan_ocr_text:segmented:unusable(no_heading_in_text_identity_from_input_job_record;ocr_columns_may_be_interleaved:fr_masthead_or_contents_text+other_item_heading_inside_body+order_opening_"}],"schema":"eo-case-bundle/v1","status":"no","text_pointers":[{"first_1500_characters":"Instrument: EO:8800\nSource: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-1941-06-26/pdf/FR-1941-06-26.pdf\nSHA-256 of the downloaded file: 879c65a331ef0c2b84ebd7c13ea68c5c53f480711e43ed53a743d3ebe6b24d3b\nLocation: 6 FR 3099, PDF pages 3-3 (confidence low)\nMachine-extracted text layer of the official PDF (OCR for 1936-1994 scans). May contain recognition errors and parts of neighbouring documents on the same pages. Not an authenticated transcription: check the PDF pages.\n\n=== PDF page 3 of 12 ===\n3099FEDERAL REGISTER, Thursday, June 26, 1941\nperform its powers and duties under, \nsaid Act, the Civil Aeronautics Board \namends the Civil Air Regulations as \nfollows:\nEffective July 1, 1941, Parts 60 and \n61 of the Civil Air Regulations are \namended as follows:\n1. By amending §  60.35 to read as \nfollows:\n§  60.35 Minimum and maximum safe  \naltitudes of flight.\n§  60.350 Minimum s a f e altitudes.  \nExclusive of taking off from or landing \nupon an airport or other landing area \naircraft shall not be flown below the fol­\nlowing minimum safe altitudes of flight:\n§  60.3500 An altitude over the con­\ngested parts of cities, towns, or settle­\nments, sufficient to permit at all times \nan emergency landing outside of such \nareas in the event of complete power \nfailure, but in no case less than 1,000 feet \nabove the ground.\n§  60.3501 An altitude over an area cer­\ntified by the Administrator as a danger \narea sufficient to permit at all times an \nemergency landing outside of such dan­\nger area in ","path":"jobs/fr-originals/output/texts/EO_8800.txt","sha256":"05f603e795a340d140bfd16a205dfaf67716b49f5